lication storage are independent configurations.\n- Exercise Gen AI Studio Playground RAG workflows (document upload, ingestion, retrieval-augmented chat) over representative small document sets.\n- Capture the inline Milvus baseline for the same operations and verify pgvector is in the same order of magnitude (dev-experimentation scope; no strict latency SLAs; flat scan without a vector index is acceptable for typical playground document sets).\n- Record recommended PostgreSQL resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n- Confirm the OGX distribution functions unchanged with the remote pgvector provider enabled (passive validation of the OGX distribution — no distribution code changes expected; the existing remote vector store implementation is reusable).\n\nACs:\n- Benchmark report exists capturing inline Milvus baseline metrics and pgvector metrics for the same representative playground RAG operations.\n- pgvector measurements are within the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline.\n- Playground RAG workflows (upload, ingestion, retrieval-augmented chat) complete successfully against the pgvector-backed OGX instance with no loss of current capability.\n- Flat scan (no vector index) is confirmed sufficient for typical playground document-set sizes, or a specific size threshold is documented where an index becomes necessary.\n- Recommended PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are recorded, with model-registry-operator sizing as the starting point.\n- OGX application storage remains on its SQLite default; no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL application-storage migration is performed or required.\n\nHLR Traceability: AC-2 (playground RAG functions), AC-4 (separate process / not inline), AC-6 (lightweight).\n\n### BRANCH-A-E003 — Implementation\nTitle: Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n\nDescription: Selected only if the go/no-go decision confirms the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164) will be available for the target release: implements PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management in that operator layer following the model-registry-operator pattern, so each Gen AI Studio playground namespace gets an auto-provisioned pgvector-backed PostgreSQL instance.\n\nScope:\n- Implement reconciliation of the pgvector PostgreSQL instance per playground namespace (per-namespace isolation) in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer — Deployment, Service, Secret, and storage using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image (no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding).\n- Follow the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL provisioning pattern for resource requests/limits, credentials, and lifecycle.\n- Expose the provisioned connection details (secret/connection string) for consumption by the gen-ai backend-for-frontend when it creates playground OGX instances.\n\nACs:\n- A playground with RAG enabled in a namespace results in an auto-provisioned PostgreSQL instance with pgvector available, with no user manual setup.\n- Instances are isolated per namespace (multi-tenant scoping).\n- Resource requests/limits start from model-registry-operator PostgreSQL sizing, adjusted per benchmark findings if needed.\n- Instance lifecycle (creation, deletion with the playground, credential handling) is managed by the operator reconciliation loop.\n- Provisioning uses the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector (registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16); no custom image is built.\n\nHLR Traceability: AC-1, AC-4, AC-5, AC-6.\n\n### BRANCH-A-E004 — Implementation\nTitle: Wire Gen AI Studio playground OGX instances to the operator-provisioned pgvector vector store\n\nDescription: Gen AI Studio gen-ai BFF changes so that playground OGXServer instances created for RAG-enabled playgrounds are configured with the remote pgvector vector_io provider pointing at the operator-provisioned PostgreSQL instance.\n\nScope:\n- Extend the gen-ai BFF's OGXServer instance creation (where storage is currently configured) to enable the remote pgvector vector_io provider (ENABLE_PGVECTOR) and consume the operator-provisioned connection details.\n- Keep OGX application storage on its existing SQLite default — only the vector_io provider configuration changes.\n- Provide a config-level rollback path: the pgvector vector_io configuration can be reverted to the prior configuration without code changes if regression is found, before inline providers are removed from the OGX distribution.\n\nACs:\n- Creating a RAG-enabled playground results in an OGX instance whose vector_io provider is remote pgvector backed by the operator-provisioned PostgreSQL instance, with no manual user configuration.\n- Playground RAG workflows function against the wired instance with no loss of current capability.\n- OGX application storage remains on the SQLite default.\n- The vector_io provider configuration is revertible without code changes (configuration-level rollback) per Rule 23 (replaces the inline provider capability).\n- Build pipeline for the changed repository is green.\n\nHLR Traceability: AC-1, AC-2, AC-4, AC-5.\n\nHmm, \"Build pipeline green\" AC — Rule 25 only mandates for konflux-onboarding in chain; but a CI-green AC is generally fine. Keep it simple: \"All changes pass the repository's existing CI/build pipeline.\" That's a reasonable AC on own deliverable.\n\n### BRANCH-B-E003 — Implementation\nTitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n\nDescription: Fallback if the operator layer is not available in time: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) when a RAG-enabled playground is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace — mirroring how the BFF currently provisions guardrails infrastructure — and wires the playground OGX instance's vector_io provider to it.\n\nScope:\n- BFF creates/manages the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret (existing Red Hat pgvector image) on RAG playground configuration, if none exists in the namespace.\n- Configure the playground OGXServer instance's remote pgvector vector_io provider to the provisioned instance.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n- Explicit lifecycle design for cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, and credential rotation (the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop).\n- Config-level rollback path.\n\nACs:\n- Configuring a playground with RAG results in a namespace-scoped PostgreSQL/pgvector instance and an OGX instance using it via the remote pgvector vector_io provider, with no manual user setup.\n- Deleting the playground cleans up the provisioned PostgreSQL resources.\n- The provisioning code path is designed so a shared platform connection string can be consumed instead of self-provisioning without a rewrite.\n- Lifecycle behaviors (pod restarts, credential rotation) are designed and documented; restart of the BFF does not orphan or duplicate instances.\n- Config-level rollback available; CI green.\n\nHLR Traceability: AC-1, AC-2, AC-4, AC-5, AC-6.\n\n### BRANCH-C-E003 — Implementation (P1)\nTitle: Bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration path for Gen AI Studio RAG\n\nDescription: Degraded fallback if neither operator-based nor BFF-managed provisioning is viable for the release: users supply their own PostgreSQL/pgvector connection details via the OGXServer custom resource spec, and the supported configuration path is validated and documented.\n\nScope:\n- Validate the OGXServer CR spec configuration path for a user-supplied PostgreSQL connection (connection details via secret).\n- Ensure the remote pgvector vector_io provider can be enabled against the user-supplied instance from playground-created OGX instances where the Playground permits it; document the manual configuration steps.\n- Document that this path does not satisfy the out-of-the-box experience and is a release fallback.\n\nACs:\n- A documented, validated configuration path exists for using an external user-provided PostgreSQL/pgvector instance with a playground OGX instance (OGXServer CR spec with connection details).\n- Setup documentation covers secret creation, CR spec fields, and enabling the pgvector vector_io provider.\n- Limitations versus the out-of-the-box experience are documented explicitly.\n\nHLR Traceability: AC-2 (partially — RAG functions with user-provided store), AC-4; AC-1 explicitly not satisfied (health warning).\n\nPriority: P1 for C. Hmm — priority inheritance check excludes branch epics. OK.\n\nNow signals per epic. Let me finalize:\n\nE001 investigation_signals:\n- question_specificity=1: three named paths + scoping model; go/no-go criteria definable.\n- source_accessibility=0: deciding fact (RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing) is an in-progress external deliverable; no readable artifact contains the answer yet.\n- local_runnability=0: the gating question (external delivery timing / release planning) cannot be exercised as a binary or library locally.\n- cluster_hardware_dependence=-1: peripheral feasibility checks (BFF-managed provisioning behavior on-cluster, operator integration) benefit from a live OpenShift cluster, but the gating question does not require one.\n- human_judgment_required=-2: the gating question is itself a go/no-go release-planning decision for the development team; the AI cannot make it.\n\nE002 ai_signals:\n- change_specificity=0: deliverable and pass/fail criteria well defined (baseline vs pgvector, order of magnitude), but no exact code surfaces for a benchmark harness.\n- pattern_precedent=0: no existing vector-store benchmark harness identified in the repos; playground RAG workflows exist to drive.\n- adapter_pattern=0: N/A.\n- existing_foundation=1: OGX already supports the remote PGVect