{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-fc0f30e7-4feb-4e10-afe5-650166442687","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-98e20250bc07fd2a","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-msic8a2n/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n\n## Description\n\nSelected only if the provisioning-approach decision rules out the operator-based path for the\nrelease (the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from the dashboard operator modularization,\nRHAISTRAT-7164, not landing in time): the Gen AI Studio gen-ai backend-for-frontend provisions the\nPostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly — similar to how the BFF currently provisions guardrails\ninfrastructure — creating the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground\nwith RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, and wiring the playground\nOGX instance's vector_io provider to it.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement BFF-managed provisioning: when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable\n  PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment,\n  Service, PVC, and Secret using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector\n  (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`; no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding).\n- Configure the playground OGXServer instance's remote pgvector vector_io provider\n  (`ENABLE_PGVECTOR`) to consume the provisioned instance; keep OGX application storage on its\n  SQLite default (vector storage and application storage are independent configurations — no\n  migration path is built).\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC becomes\n  available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF must be able to consume the shared connection string rather than\n  self-provisioning, without a rewrite of this feature.\n- Address lifecycle management explicitly — the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop:\n  cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts (no orphaned or duplicate instances),\n  and credential rotation need explicit design and tests.\n- Scope instances per namespace (per-namespace isolation is the preferred multi-tenant model;\n  confirm against the decision record's scoping outcome).\n- Provide a configuration-level rollback path: the pgvector vector_io configuration must be\n  revertible to the prior configuration without code changes if a regression is found, preserving a\n  safety margin before inline providers are removed from the OGX distribution.\n- Document that the default store is positioned for development experimentation; users with\n  production-scale vector workloads are directed to an external vector database.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Configuring a playground with RAG results in a namespace-scoped PostgreSQL/pgvector instance and\n  an OGX instance using it via the remote pgvector vector_io provider, with no manual user setup\n  steps.\n- Deleting the playground cleans up the provisioned PostgreSQL resources (Deployment, Service, PVC,\n  Secret).\n- Restarting the BFF neither orphans nor duplicates the provisioned instance; lifecycle behavior\n  for pod restarts and credential rotation is implemented and tested.\n- The provisioning code path is structured so a shared platform connection string can be consumed\n  instead of self-provisioning without a rewrite (portability to the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided\n  database).\n- OGX application storage remains on the SQLite default; only the vector_io provider configuration\n  changes.\n- The pgvector vector_io configuration can be reverted to the prior configuration without code\n  changes (configuration-level rollback).\n- Gen AI Studio Playground RAG workflows function against the provisioned instance with no loss of\n  current capability, and all changes pass the odh-dashboard repository's existing build and CI\n  pipeline.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning)** — delivered by\n  BFF self-provisioning.\n- **AC-2 (Playground RAG workflows function, no loss of capability)** — provisioning and wiring are\n  bundled here because they edit the same BFF code paths and configuration surface; splitting would\n  double-touch the same call sites.\n- **AC-4 (separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance)** — the PostgreSQL instance is an\n  external process in the namespace, not an inline provider.\n- **AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation)** — namespace-scoped provisioning.\n- **AC-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments)** — sizing starts from the\n  model-registry operator's PostgreSQL references, adjusted per the pgvector benchmark findings.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":77690,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a69fe9ec9252deb2","agent_id":"a890b7bc884117537","session_id":"b07b26f1-905e-4b64-8357-872b48ad7853","uuid":"7022efda-61a3-432f-b1c9-8bf2f89c6f0a","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:25:36.997Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
