{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d76b990134cbd36","type":"tool_result","content":".context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ai4rag.md\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md\n---\n**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of ModelRegistry, Catalog, and AIHub custom resources, provisioning model-registry service instances with kube-rbac-proxy authentication, PostgreSQL storage, and Gateway API or OpenShift Route ingress. [source: cmd/modelregistry.go:169, internal/controller/modelregistry_controller.go:282, internal/controller/modelcatalog_controller.go:183]\n**Detailed**: The model-registry-operator is a controller-runtime-based Kubernetes operator that reconciles three CRDs — ModelRegistry (v1alpha1/v1beta1), Catalog (v1alpha1), and AIHub (v1alpha1) — to provision and manage model registry service instances within the RHOAI platform. For each ModelRegistry CR, the operator creates a Deployment running the model-registry proxy backed by either PostgreSQL or MySQL storage, with an optional kube-rbac-proxy sidecar that enforces SubjectAccessReview-based authorization on the HTTPS port. The operator manages the full resource lifecycle including Services, ServiceAccounts, RBAC Roles/Bindings, Secrets, NetworkPolicies, and ingress resources (Gateway API HTTPRoutes or OpenShift Routes depending on cluster capabilities). The ModelCatalog reconciler similarly provisions a centralized catalog service with its own PostgreSQL instance. The operator detects cluster capabilities at startup — including OpenShift APIs, BYOIDC mode (where user.openshift.io is absent), Gateway API availability, and the platform Auth CRD — to conditionally create platform-specific resources. [source: internal/controller/util.go:36-39, internal/controller/config/defaults.go:197-211, internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:220-272]\n| model-catalog-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgresql ({image}) |\n| {registry-name}-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgres ({image}) |\n| model-catalog-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n---\n**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\nThe odh-dashboard follows a micro-frontend architecture using Webpack Module Federation, where a host React application dynamically loads domain-specific UI modules (gen-ai, model-registry, notebooks, eval-hub, agent-ops, automl, autorag, data-registry, maas, mlflow) at runtime via `/_mf/:name/*` paths. Each UI module is paired with a dedicated Go BFF sidecar that handles its backend operations, creating a vertically-sliced architecture where each domain owns its full request path from UI through BFF to Kubernetes API. The federation configuration (in `manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml`) maps path prefixes to sidecar ports, enabling a single ingress point to fan out to domain-specific backends. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n| gen-ai BFF | Go BFF Sidecar | Federated gen-ai backend-for-frontend service |\n| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile.workspace:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n---\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/odh-dashboard.md\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md\n0","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a98399be128ee0ab","agent_id":"a4d878f68285fa218","session_id":"b07b26f1-905e-4b64-8357-872b48ad7853","uuid":"59124f7c-a112-4a23-bf3b-58489ecd10f7","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:28:50.827Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d76b990134cbd36","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
