{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7358f9455d268ed","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: 'human_process_gates is scored 0 for BRANCH-A-E003 and BRANCH-C-E003\n10\t    (rationale: no gates beyond normal code/docs review) but 1 for BRANCH-A-E004 and\n11\t    BRANCH-B-E003 (rationale: none beyond normal code review) — the same condition\n12\t    receives different values across sibling epics; recalibrate to one convention.'\n13\t- severity: minor\n14\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n15\t  description: BRANCH-A-E003 scope includes verifying disconnected-install coverage\n16\t    of the PostgreSQL image (the un-corroborated rhods-operator CSV RELATED_IMAGE\n17\t    claim), but no acceptance criterion covers that verification — add an AC for the\n18\t    disconnected-install verification or move the item to Risks/Notes.\n19\terror: null\n20\t---\n21\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8004\n22\t\n23\t## Review Summary\n24\t\n25\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n26\tRecommendation: accept\n27\t\n28\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8004` exited 0 — body sections,\n29\tcanonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type\n30\tparallelism, and epic-size triggers all pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full\n31\tdecomposition), so the 6-epic multi-branch output is expected and not penalized.\n32\t\n33\tThe strategy body references an unavailable strategy attachment; the decomposer used the Acceptance\n34\tCriteria bullets as surrogate HLRs (AC-1..AC-6) and recorded the missing sections as a health\n35\twarning — a reasonable and transparent handling. All nine Staff Engineer Input directives are\n36\ttraceably applied and documented in the summary's override table.\n37\t\n38\t## Criterion Details\n39\t\n40\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n41\tTraceability matrix present in the summary. AC-1, AC-2, AC-4, AC-5, AC-6 all map to at least one\n42\tepic (each conditional branch carries its own traceability section). AC-3 (Auto Rag) is explicitly\n43\trecorded as out of scope with justification (Staff Engineer directive #5 — Auto Rag compatibility\n44\tis RHAISTRAT-7824's scope for RHOAI 3.6), which is a justified deferral, not a gap. Priority\n45\tinheritance is clean: the gating Investigation E001 is P0; no lower-priority epic blocks P0 work\n46\t(BRANCH-C-E003 is P1 and nothing P0 depends on it — the summary documents this as intentional).\n47\tNo priority collapse: the branch epics are mutually exclusive alternatives, not deferrable\n48\tfeatures bundled into P0 epics. AC-2's bundling into each branch's wiring epic is justified (same\n49\tconfiguration surface; splitting would double-touch the same call sites).\n50\t\n51\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n52\tDAG is a tree rooted at E001 with one chain in branch A. Every diagram edge has a matching\n53\tfrontmatter `dependencies` entry and vice versa (E001→A-E003, E001→B-E003, E001→C-E003,\n54\tA-E003→A-E004); arrows run dependency→dependent per convention. No circular dependencies, no\n55\tedges to nonexistent epics, and no main-plan epic depends on a branch epic. All three branch files\n56\tset both `branch` (A/B/C, matching filenames) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`, and each\n57\t`gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`. The A-E003→A-E004 edge is a\n58\tCRD/API producer→consumer edge (provisioning API + connection secret), not a configuration\n59\treference — correctly an edge. E002's deliberate isolation is justified: it stands up its own\n60\tmanually provisioned pgvector instance (\"does not depend on any product provisioning path\") and\n61\tits sizing findings feed the provisioning epics as AC guidance, which is the correct non-edge\n62\tshape. Implementation-type