{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-891bfe33148c679e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 1\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio)\n11\t\n12\t## Input Notes\n13\t\n14\tThe strategy body states the full strategy exceeds the Jira description size limit and is stored as\n15\tan attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`); that attachment is not provisioned in this\n16\tenvironment. The decomposition is therefore derived from the strategy summary, problem statement,\n17\tacceptance criteria, open questions, and the **Staff Engineer Input** section — which is non-empty\n18\tand takes precedence per the decomposition contract. The Acceptance Criteria bullets are used as\n19\tsurrogate HLRs (AC-1..AC-6 below); the missing Affected Components / Impacted Teams / prioritized\n20\tHLR / Dependencies / NFR tables are recorded as a health warning.\n21\t\n22\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (traceability)\n23\t\n24\tEach override applied, and where it changed the decomposition:\n25\t\n26\t| # | Staff Engineer directive | Effect on decomposition |\n27\t|---|--------------------------|-------------------------|\n28\t| 1 | OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL (SQLite is the default; the gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite). Introducing PostgreSQL is new work. | PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning is modeled as new implementation work (branch epics), not an extension of existing infrastructure. |\n29\t| 2 | A Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector already exists (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`); model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it; no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding. | No image-build, `repo-onboarding`, or `konflux-onboarding` epics generated. Provisioning epics reference the existing image. |\n30\t| 3 | Platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC will not be available until RHOAI 3.6; for 3.5 Gen AI Studio must provision its own PostgreSQL. | Self-provisioning (operator-based or BFF-managed) is the 3.5 deliverable; portability to the 3.6 shared database is a scope item / AC on the BFF-managed path. |\n31\t| 4 | RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) is a hard dependency for operator-based self-provisioning; fallbacks are bring-your-own PostgreSQL or BFF-managed provisioning; the dev team should define a go/no-go gate in sprint planning. | Modeled as a gating Investigation with three bounded outcomes → conditional decomposition branches A (operator-based), B (BFF-managed), C (bring-your-own). |\n32\t| 5 | Decouple from Auto Rag — Auto Rag compatibility is RHAISTRAT-7824's scope (RHOAI 3.6). | Auto Rag removed from scope, ACs, and validation. AC-3 is recorded as out-of-scope in the HLR Traceability Matrix. The per-instance scoping motivation for Auto Rag is removed; per-namespace is the preferred model. |\n33\t| 6 | Performance is dev-experimentation scope: no strict latency SLAs; sprint-1 benchmark verifies order-of-magnitude parity with the inline Milvus baseline; flat scan (no index) is sufficient. | The benchmark epic carries order-of-magnitude parity and flat-scan ACs; strategy open questions about latency thresholds, corpus size ranges, and index type are removed (not turned into epics). |\n34\t| 7 | PostgreSQL resource sizing starts from model-registry-operator's requests/limits. | Sizing recorded as an AC on the benchmark and provisioning epics, not a separate epic. |\n35\t| 8 | Existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable; the work is infrastructure/provisioning. | No OGX vector_io provider-integration epic; ogx-distribution is treated as a passive component (validation ACs only). |\n36\t| 9 | Vector storage (`remote::pgvector` via ENABLE_PGVECTOR) and OGX application storage (SQLite) are independent configurations; no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concern. | All wiring epics scope to the vector_io provider only; \"no application-storage migration\" is an explicit AC. |\n37\t\n38\t## Component Mapping Notes\n39\t\n40\t- **Gen AI Studio** — the gen-ai backend-for-frontend (BFF) and playground RAG workflows live in the\n41\t  odh-dashboard repository (`packages/gen-ai/bff`); canonical component `Gen AI Studio` assigned to\n42\t  the decision, benchmark, and BFF wiring/provisioning epics.\n43\t- **AI Core Dashboard** — the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered by the dashboard operator\n44\t  modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164); canonical component `AI Core Dashboard` assigned to the\n45\t  operator-based provisioning epic. This epic's work spans the odh-dashboard repository's operator\n46\t  sub-system; the single most-relevant canonical name is the dashboard component.\n47\t- **OGX Core (ogx-distribution)** — **passive component**: the distribution already ships the\n48\t  remote PGVector vector_io backend (corroborated by `ogx-distribution.md`, which lists PGVector as\n49\t  a vector I/O integration point and documents that inline providers are stripped). No distribution\n50\t  code changes are expected; validation that OGX functions unchanged with the remote pgvector\n51\t  provider enabled is an acceptance criterion on the benchmark epic. No separate validation epic is\n52\t  generated (no evidence a different team validates it — the Impacted Teams table is unavailable).\n53\t\n54\t## Epic List\n55\t\n56\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n57\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n58\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Decide the provisioning approach for the Gen AI Studio default pgvector vector store | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n59\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Benchmark pgvector and validate Gen AI Studio Playground RAG workflows | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n60\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds | Implementation | AI Core Dashboard | AI Core Dashboard | P0 |\n61\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 | Wire Gen AI Studio playground OGX instances to the operator-provisioned pgvector vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n62\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E003 | Bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration path for Gen AI Studio RAG | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P1 |\n64\t\n65\tBranch epics are conditional alternatives selected by the outcome of the gating Investigation\n66\t(RHAISTRAT-8004-E001); exactly one branch is scheduled. `epic_count` counts all epics including\n67\tbranches.\n68\t\n69\t## Dependency DAG\n70\t\n71\t```mermaid\n72\tgraph TD\n73\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E001<br/>Provisioning approach go/no-go<br/>(Investigation)\"]\n74\t    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E002<br/>pgvector benchmark + Playground RAG validation\"]\n75\t    BR_A_E003[\"BRANCH-A-E003<br/>Operator-based pgvector provisioning\"]\n76\t    BR_A_E004[\"BRANCH-A-E004<br/>BFF wiring to operator-provisioned store\"]\n77\t    BR_B_E003[\"BRANCH-B-E003<br/>BFF-managed pgvector provisioning\"]\n78\t    BR_C_E003[\"BRANCH-C-E003<br/>Bring-your-own PostgreSQL path\"]\n79\t\n80\t    E001 --> BR_A_E003\n81\t    E001 --> BR_B_E003\n82\t    E001 --> BR_C_E003\n83\t    BR_A_E003 --> BR_A_E004\n84\t```\n85\t\n86\tArrows point from dependency to dependent. The benchmark epic (E002) is deliberately unconnected:\n87\tit is Tier 1 risk-reduction work that does not depend on the provisioning decision (any\n88\tpgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance suffices for the benchmark), and its sizing findings feed the\n89\tprovisioning epics as acceptance-criteria guidance, not as a blocking edge. Branch epics are\n90\texcluded from the main-plan critical path; the main plan (E001, E002, no edges between them) has\n91\tcritical path length 1. Within branch A the chain E001 → BRANCH-A-E003 → BRANCH-A-E004 has length 3\n92\tif that branch is selected.\n93\t\n94\t## DAG Justification\n95\t\n96\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n97\t|------|------|-----------|\n98\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The operator-based provisioning epic exists only if the go/no-go confirms the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (RHAISTRAT-7164) lands in time. True gate: `remove` — the epic does not exist if another outcome is selected. |\n99\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E003 | Rule 3 | The BFF-managed provisioning epic exists only if the go/no-go selects the BFF fallback. True gate: `remove`. |\n100\t| E001 → BRANCH-C-E003 | Rule 3 | The bring-your-own path exists only if the go/no-go selects the degraded fallback. True gate: `remove`. |\n101\t| BRANCH-A-E003 → BRANCH-A-E004 | Rule 12/13 (framework/CRD producer → consumer serial) | The BFF wiring builds against the provisioning API and provisioned connection the operator epic delivers (a CRD/API surface, not a configuration reference such as an image digest). |\n102\t| (no edge) E002 ↔ anything | Rules 14/17 + Step 5.5 | The benchmark runs in a different work stream (validation vs. provisioning), is independent of the selected provisioning path, and delivers Tier 1 risk-reduction value on its own. Its coupling to provisioning (resource sizing) is expressed as an AC on the provisioning epics, never as a DAG edge. |\n103\t\n104\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n105\t\n106\tThe prioritized HLR table is in the unavailable strategy attachment; the strategy's Acceptance\n107\tCriteria bullets serve as surrogate HLRs (strategy frontmatter priority is Blocker → all P0).\n108\t\n109\t| HLR (surrogate) | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n110\t|-----------------|-------------|---------|-------|\n111\t| AC-1 | Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning | BRANCH-A-E003 + BRANCH-A-E004; BRANCH-B-E003 | Not satisfied by BRANCH-C-E003 — see Health Warnings. |\n112\t| AC-2 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with no loss of capability | E002 (validation); BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E003, BRANCH-C-E003 (wiring/config) | Bundled into each branch's wiring epic: the workflow validation and the wiring edit the same BFF/OGX configuration surface — splitting would double-touch the same call sites. |\n113\t| AC-3 | Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | — (out of scope) | **Removed per Staff Engineer Input**: Auto Rag compatibility is RHAISTRAT-7824's scope (RHOAI 3.6). Logged override #5. |\n114\t| AC-4 | Vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance | E002 (validation); BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003, BRANCH-C-E003 | All branches provision/consume an external PostgreSQL process; no inline provider. |\n115\t| AC-5 | Multi-tenant scoping (e.g., per-namespace isolation) | E001 (scoping decision); BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E003 | Per-namespace is the preferred model (staff input); the decision epic confirms it now that the Auto Rag per-instance motivation is out of scope. |\n116\t| AC-6 | Lightweight for resource-constrained environments | E002 (benchmark + sizing); BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 | Sizing starts from model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits, adjusted per benchmark findings. |\n117\t\n118\tOpen questions from the strategy, resolved per staff input rather than turned into epics:\n119\t- Scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance): folded into the gating Investigation's decision\n120\t  record (same deliverable — do not split investigations for the sake of splitting).\n121\t- Migration path to production-grade vector databases: not a gating unknown — the default store is\n122\t  positioned as development-experimentation scope and production-scale workloads are directed to\n123\t  external vector databases; recorded as guidance/ACs on the branch epics, not an epic.\n124\t- Latency thresholds, corpus size ranges, index type selection: removed per staff override #6.\n125\t\n126\t## Health Warnings\n127\t\n128\t- **Missing strategy sections:** the full strategy (Affected Components, Impacted Teams, prioritized\n129\t  HLRs, Dependencies, NFRs) is in an attachment not available in this environment. Team assignments\n130\t  and HLR priorities are inferred from the summary, acceptance criteria, and Staff Engineer Input;\n131\t  the strategy's Impacted Teams table should be used to correct team assignments at scheduling\n132\t  time. The team value `Gen AI Studio` (BFF/benchmark/decision epics) and `AI Core Dashboard`\n133\t  (operator epic) are best-effort canonical mappings.\n134\t- **Fallback fails the primary acceptance criterion (scope trap):** BRANCH-C-E003 (bring-your-own\n135\t  PostgreSQL) cannot satisfy AC-1 (out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning). If the go/no-go selects\n136\t  that branch, the strategy's primary AC fails for the release and must be re-negotiated with the\n137\t  stakeholder — this is a deliberate, staff-engineer-sanctioned fallback, but reviewers should note\n138\t  that branch C ships a degraded experience, which is why it is P1 while the branches that satisfy\n139\t  AC-1 are P0.\n140\t- **Unverified CSV claim (architecture claim not corroborated):** the Staff Engineer Input states\n141\t  `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` is already present in the rhods-operator CSV for disconnected\n142\t  installs. The architecture context for rhods-operator contains no PostgreSQL or RELATED_IMAGE\n143\t  references, so this claim could not be corroborated. Disconnected-install coverage of the\n144\t  PostgreSQL image should be verified during implementation — recorded as an acceptance criterion\n145\t  on BRANCH-A-E003 (operator-based provisioning) so the verification outcome is checkable.\n146\t- **Benchmark is a premise check, not a gate:** the pgvector benchmark (E002) runs in parallel with\n147\t  the provisioning decision rather than gating it, per staff direction (dev-experimentation scope,\n148\t  order-of-magnitude expectation, small document sets). If the benchmark finds pgvector more than an\n149\t  order of magnitude off the inline Milvus baseline, the strategy premise itself is in question and\n150\t  the decomposition must be revisited — this is a risk, not a planned branch.\n151\t- **Epic sizes:** all epics are within the ~2-week guideline; the branch implementations are\n152\t  focused, single-surface changes (operator reconciliation, BFF provisioning/wiring, or a\n153\t  configuration path).\n154\t- **Priority inversion note (non-blocking):** BRANCH-C-E003 is P1 while its gating decision and the\n155\t  other branches are P0 — intentional, because branch C defers AC-1 and is only scheduled if both\n156\t  self-provisioning paths are ruled out.\n157\t\n158\t## Tiered Delivery\n159\t\n160\t- **Tier 1 (independent of RHAISTRAT-7164 timing, starts immediately):**\n161\t  - RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 — the provisioning-approach go/no-go decision (tracks the external\n162\t    dependency; produces the decision record regardless of outcome).\n163\t  - RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 — the pgvector benchmark and Playground RAG validation (risk-reduction\n164\t    spike; requires only a manually provisioned pgvector instance for testing).\n165\t- **Tier 2 (gated by the E001 outcome, transitively by RHAISTRAT-7164 timing):**\n166\t  - Branch A (operator-based provisioning + BFF wiring) — preferred if RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time.\n167\t  - Branch B (BFF-managed provisioning) — fallback that removes the external dependency.\n168\t  - Branch C (bring-your-own) — last-resort degraded fallback.\n169\t\n170\tPartial value without the external dependency: Tier 1 resolves the decision and de-risks the\n171\tpgvector choice; branch B (and C) can each deliver the out-of-the-box experience without\n172\tRHAISTRAT-7164, which is why the dependency has bounded fallbacks rather than a linear block.\n173\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9636ee73fa8fca7","agent_id":"a3983a4f5ffc7ae27","session_id":"b07b26f1-905e-4b64-8357-872b48ad7853","uuid":"26292cd1-b9a1-492a-bd06-5ae864cec80f","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:33:40.138Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-891bfe33148c679e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
