{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98bd80f548f02dee","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n3\ttitle: Decide the provisioning approach for the Gen AI Studio default pgvector vector\n4\t  store\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tdependencies: []\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Low\n20\tai_implementability_score: -2\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Decide the provisioning approach for the Gen AI Studio default pgvector vector store\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis investigation resolves the one genuine unknown that gates all provisioning implementation for\n31\tthe default vector store strategy: which of three bounded paths Gen AI Studio uses to make a\n32\tPostgreSQL/pgvector instance available to playground RAG workflows in the target RHOAI release —\n33\toperator-based provisioning in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered by the dashboard\n34\toperator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164), BFF-managed self-provisioning by the gen-ai\n35\tbackend-for-frontend, or a bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration path for users. It also confirms\n36\tthe scoping model for the default store (per-namespace vs per-instance isolation). The Staff\n37\tEngineer Input directs the development team to define a go/no-go gate for this decision as part of\n38\tsprint planning; this investigation produces that decision.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- Track the delivery status of RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) against the\n43\t  RHOAI 3.5 timeline. It is a hard dependency for the operator-based path: until it delivers, there\n44\t  is no RHOAI-controlled operator where PostgreSQL lifecycle management can live. The upstream\n45\t  ogx-k8s-operator cannot carry RHOAI-specific provisioning logic.\n46\t- Define the go/no-go criteria for the release decision: the latest date at which each path remains\n47\t  viable for the release, and the evidence each path needs (operator layer availability, BFF\n48\t  provisioning design feasibility, acceptance of a degraded bring-your-own experience).\n49\t- Evaluate the three provisioning paths against: lifecycle management (cleanup on playground\n50\t  deletion, pod-restart handling, credential rotation — the BFF is not an operator with a\n51\t  reconciliation loop), multi-tenant isolation, and portability to the platform-provided shared\n52\t  PostgreSQL managed via the DSC that is planned for RHOAI 3.6 (for 3.5, Gen AI Studio must\n53\t  provision its own PostgreSQL instance).\n54\t- Confirm the scoping model for the default vector store. Per-namespace isolation is the preferred\n55\t  model; the per-instance motivation came from Auto Rag, which is out of scope for this strategy\n56\t  (Auto Rag compatibility is addressed in RHAISTRAT-7824 for RHOAI 3.6).\n57\t- Produce a decision record that names the selected provisioning path, the go/no-go criteria, the\n58\t  evidence used, and the confirmed scoping model, and hand it to sprint planning for scheduling of\n59\t  the corresponding implementation work.\n60\t\n61\t## Acceptance Criteria\n62\t\n63\t- A written decision record exists that names the selected provisioning path (operator-based,\n64\t  BFF-managed, or bring-your-own PostgreSQL) together with the go/no-go criteria and the evidence\n65\t  used to select it.\n66\t- The record states the assessed delivery status of RHAISTRAT-7164 and the date or trigger after\n67\t  which each fallback path applies for the release.\n68\t- The scoping model (per-namespace or per-instance isolation) for the default vector store is\n69\t  confirmed and recorded in the decision record.\n70\t- The decision, including its implications for the out-of-the-box experience if the\n71\t  bring-your-own fallback is selected, is presented at sprint planning and the outcome is recorded.\n72\t\n73\t## HLR Traceability\n74\t\n75\t- **AC-5 (multi-tenant scoping, e.g. per-namespace isolation)** — the scoping model is decided here\n76\t  and inherited by the provisioning implementations.\n77\t- **AC-1 (out-of-the-box default vector store)** — this decision determines which provisioning\n78\t  implementation can satisfy the no-manual-setup requirement for the release.\n79\t- **AC-4 (separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance)** — the decision record confirms every\n80\t  candidate path provisions an external PostgreSQL process rather than an inline provider.\n81\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9636ee73fa8fca7","agent_id":"a3983a4f5ffc7ae27","session_id":"b07b26f1-905e-4b64-8357-872b48ad7853","uuid":"181bb1fe-d3ca-4a1d-af20-cd3269ee278c","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:33:43.609Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-98bd80f548f02dee","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
