{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4af13b8b2d008b3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 (Implementation)\n2\t\n3\t| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n4\t|--------|-------|-----------|\n5\t| change_specificity | 0 | The deliverable and pass/fail criteria are well defined (inline Milvus baseline vs pgvector, order-of-magnitude parity, flat scan for small document sets), but no exact code surfaces exist for a benchmark harness — the harness design is open. |\n6\t| pattern_precedent | 0 | No existing vector-store benchmark harness was identified in the odh-dashboard or ogx-distribution repositories; the playground RAG workflows that drive the benchmark do exist. |\n7\t| adapter_pattern | 0 | N/A — no reference implementation is being followed; this is a new validation harness. |\n8\t| existing_foundation | 1 | Strong foundation: OGX already supports the remote PGVector vector_io backend, the playground RAG workflows exist, and the Staff Engineer Input states the existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable. |\n9\t| open_questions | 1 | All performance expectations are resolved by the Staff Engineer Input: dev-experimentation scope, no strict latency SLAs, order-of-magnitude parity check, flat scan acceptable — the former latency/corpus/index open questions were removed. |\n10\t| external_dependency | 1 | None: the Red Hat PostgreSQL image with pgvector already exists, and all components are in-platform (OGX distribution, odh-dashboard). |\n11\t| human_process_gates | 1 | None — the benchmark report needs no human approval step. |\n12\t| repo_access | 1 | ogx-distribution and odh-dashboard repositories are readable and modifiable. |\n13\t| architecture_claims | 1 | `ogx-distribution.md` corroborates the premise: PGVector is a listed vector I/O integration point (SQL client, 5432/TCP), the distribution strips inline providers and ships remote-capable providers, and the