it on the wrong side of their own rationale text.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy and staff input. Rule-mandated ACs are\npresent where applicable: both wiring/provisioning epics that replace the inline vector store\ncarry configuration-level rollback ACs (BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E003; BRANCH-C documents\nrevertibility); the disconnected-install verification AC on BRANCH-A-E003 makes the\nuncorroborated CSV claim checkable; build/CI ACs appear on the odh-dashboard epics. No\nexternally-controlled precondition is misfiled as an AC (E001's \"presented at sprint planning\"\nis the epic's own deliverable — producing and presenting the decision record — not access\ngranted by others). No bare draft-ID references in any epic body.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll in-scope strategy capabilities are accounted for: out-of-the-box default store (branches\nA/B), separate-process decoupling (all branches + E002 validation), multi-tenant scoping (E001\ndecision + branch implementation), lightweight/resource sizing (E002 benchmark + branch ACs),\nand no-regression validation (E002 + wiring ACs). The conditional branches cover all three\nbounded outcomes named by the Staff Engineer Input. Strategy-level context is carried forward,\nnot dropped: the RHAISTRAT-7164 hard dependency and fallbacks structure the whole DAG; the\nuncorroborated rhods-operator CSV claim, the branch-C scope trap, and the benchmark-is-a-premise-\ncheck risk are all recorded as health warnings with checkable consequences. The missing strategy\nattachment (Affected Components / Impacted Teams / prioritized HLRs) is explicitly recorded as a\nhealth warning with surrogate HLRs — the correct handling of unavailable input. No\nDocumentation Support declaration exists in the available strategy text, and the validator\nconfirms Rule 11 coverage, so no docs-authoring epic is required.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_